  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 251 PM EDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nBroad upper-level troughing currently resides over the northern \nCONUS with an embedded shortwave over the northern Plains. Weak and \nelongated surface troughing induced by the shortwave is also located \nover the northern Plains. A cold front has slowly been moving \nsouthward, triggering convection upstream across Iowa and northern \nIllinois. Though the front has been advancing towards Indiana, it \nhas slowed in its forward progress today.\n\nGuidance is in fairly good agreement showing this front continuing \nto slow, or even stall, to our west. Multiple rounds of showers and \nthunderstorms are depicted by CAM guidance, particularly where the \nnocturnal low-level jet terminates. This places the most likely area \nof thunderstorm activity far to our west over Missouri into central \nIllinois. A large complex of thunderstorms is most likely to \ndevelop, moving eastward into a dry air mass with little large-scale \nforcing. As such, weakening is anticipated with any storm complex \nthat tries to enter Indiana from Illinois.\n\nNevertheless, repeated rounds of convection over Illinois should \nallow for atmospheric saturation which may enable shower and storm \nactivity to finally advance into Indiana tomorrow morning. Even if \nit does, it still looks to be in a weakening state and likely just a \nremnant area of stratiform rain or showers. With little instability \nand shear, severe weather is not anticipated through Thursday \nmorning.\n\nRenewed thunderstorm activity is possible on Thursday afternoon but \na key mitigating factor may be too much to overcome. This would be \nthe lingering cold pool / stabilization from tonight's convective \ncomplex. Most guidance is against the idea of afternoon showers and \nstorms on Thursday. However, a few do show development after sunset \nThursday evening as the low-level jet tries to kick in to our \nsouthwest.\n\nA very similar situation may unfold on Friday with isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms. Forcing looks minimal, so \nconfidence is fairly low on coverage.\n\nThis Weekend Into Next Week\n\nA semi-active period of weather may develop this weekend into next \nweek as ridging over the intermountain west amplifies, displacing \nthe CONUS trough eastward. Northwest flow takes hold over Indiana \nwith a warm and humid air mass at the surface. Repeated rounds of \nthunderstorm development upstream could propagate southeastward \naided by the upper-level flow aloft. Additionally, nightly low-level \njet development could allow for convective maintenance downstream. \nUncertainty is still high, however, even if the environment is \nfavorable for MCSs (mesoscale convective systems). This is because \nforcing in this kind of environment can be fairly weak and nebulous. \nPin-pointing exactly where thunderstorms will develop can be tricky, \nand which clusters become the dominant MCS even trickier. \nAdditionally, subsequent convective development depends on how the \nprevious round of convection altered the overall environment.",
